epo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n- **daily-backup** ×2 (2026-10-03 23:53 → 2026-10-04 23:20 UTC): 2 error(s): repo-behind-blocked:global-memory, repo-push:dotclaude - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\n- **adjudicate-budget-tripped** ×1 (2026-10-04 13:00 → 2026-10-04 13:00 UTC): stopped after 335,350 / 250,000 tokens\n\nFull log: `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. Surface this once; clear the file after the user has seen it and the cause is handled.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (3526 chars, updated 2026-10-05 11:47)","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7048e5a8-c696-45d6-a779-d96f55aa6999","timestamp":"2026-10-05T12:43:52.061Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nMonday, October 05, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n320 compiled articles (237 concepts, 82 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\nLatest: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/2026-10-04.md`. Not loaded here; read it only when a question needs that day.\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Slate CLEARED 10/3; registry 171 rows, 7-day rule, /landed; NEXT design PM system →`project-triage/docs/STATUS.md`\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: W5 ASU done, W1-4 backfilled; `pff_game_result.py`; bye wk = mid-season →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: Supertraining Ch1-7 in vault (10/3); NEXT Ch8 on Mac →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n- **daily-backup** ×2 (2026-10-03 23:53 → 2026-10-04 23:20 UTC): 2 error(s): repo-behind-blocked:global-memory, repo-push:dotclaude - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\n- **adjudicate-budget-tripped** ×1 (2026-10-04 13:00 → 2026-10-04 13:00 UTC): stopped after 335,350 / 250,000 tokens\n\nFull log: `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. Surface this once; clear the file after the user has seen it and the cause is handled.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (3526 chars, updated 2026-10-05 11:47)\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"67004755-6eb9-4324-a41f-97820a746f73","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
