{"parentUuid":"ee376c34-b88a-4fc8-8b0a-93a07dd6db17","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"54d18546-f674-4845-9b9d-743f766d3812","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7wRpZXNtUmencUj1dM9kq","content":"Launching skill: closeout"}]},"uuid":"968b3666-9a39-44c3-8fb8-10384aaff652","timestamp":"2026-10-05T13:44:11.445Z","toolUseResult":{"success":true,"commandName":"closeout"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ee376c34-b88a-4fc8-8b0a-93a07dd6db17","session_id":"67004755-6eb9-4324-a41f-97820a746f73","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"67004755-6eb9-4324-a41f-97820a746f73","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"968b3666-9a39-44c3-8fb8-10384aaff652","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"54d18546-f674-4845-9b9d-743f766d3812","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/closeout\n\n# Closeout\n\nOne command that replaces the manual end-of-day sequence: \"any loose ends?\" → \"commit and push what needs to be\" → \"is this in memory?\" → \"write a handoff so Codex can pick this up.\"\n\nRun all five checks, then give ONE verdict. Do not ask permission between steps — the invocation of this skill IS the authorization for the full sequence. Commit and push is standing-authorized for every DEV repo: each repo's `AGENTS.md` Session routine says \"update STATUS, commit, push at end\", and unpushed work on one machine is the top cause of conflicts on the other. The only exceptions are repos whose `CLAUDE.md` says their data must stay local (DexaProject is the precedent); commit those locally and list them as open.\n\nThis one file serves Claude Code, Codex and Cowork (uploaded to claude.ai). Cowork has no TaskList and no Claude Code memory hooks: do what applies there and say what was skipped.\n\n## Step 1 — Loose-ends sweep\n\n- `git status` across **every** repo in DEV, not only the ones named in this conversation. Another session, another harness, or the other machine may have left work dirty, and a closeout that only checks what it remembers touching will say \"clear to sign off\" over someone else's uncommitted work. Walk `DEV/`, `DEV/Football/` and `DEV/SpecialRequests/`, and report any repo with uncommitted or unpushed commits. The full walk takes about 90 seconds.\n  - Windows: `git -C <repo> status --porcelain` per repo. Do NOT use `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` to look for files; it silently skips paths past the 260-character limit. Inside a repo, `git ls-files` is the reliable way to ask whether a file exists.\n  - Repos with dirty state you did not touch: commit and push them like any other, and name them in the report so the user knows another session left them.\n- **Registry drift.** Run `python <dir of portfolio_json>/portfolio.py crosswalk` (`portfolio_json` is in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.<platform>.json`). It compares `portfolio.json` with every DEV folder and Drive `01_PROJECTS` folder and only reports; it never moves anything. Do not fix drift during closeout. List each item it found under \"Still open\", naming any folder created this session first: that is a project started without a row, the exact way projects got lost before the 2026-10-03 organization pass. `drive_unreachable` means the Drive checks never ran; say so, do not call it clean. Cowork cannot run it; say it was skipped.\n- **7-day rule.** Run `python <dir of portfolio_json>/portfolio.py seven-day --apply`. Name any project it paused, and any project on its last two days, under \"Still open\" with what it still needs (a receiver, step 1). Revive with the board's status pill or `portfolio.py` once there is a first step to show.\n- Unfinished todos/tasks from this session (TaskList if available).\n- Background jobs or workflows still running.\n- Anything the user was promised earlier in the session that wasn't delivered.\n\n## Step 2 — Commit & push\n\n- **Registry edits get their own commit.** In project-triage, if `git status --porcelain portfolio.json` shows a change (board edits from the day, `set-status`, `set-receiver`), commit that file alone first as `data: board edits <YYYY-MM-DD>`, then the rest. The board header's \"N unpushed registry edits\" counts these; a skipped one becomes a JSON merge conflict when the other machine edits too.\n- Every repo touched this session: stage, commit with a descriptive message, `git pull --no-rebase` (the other machine may have pushed), push. If the merge conflicts, follow the conflict steps in `dev-sync/SKILL.md` (they are the one home for them), then push and list the file under \"Still open\".\n- Before staging, make sure any new file over 95 MB and any `.env` is in `.gitignore`.\n- Local-only repos (CLAUDE.md says so): commit, do not push, list it.\n- If `~/.claude/mods` changed this session (a mod added, edited or `.disabled`), run the mods sync (`bash ~/.claude/mods/mods-sync.sh` on the Mac, `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $HOME\\.claude\\mods\\mods-sync.ps1` on Windows) and list any invalid mod under \"Still open\".\n- Also check the three config repos, `~/.claude` (dotclaude), `~/.claude/skills` (claude-skills) and `~/.claude/global-memory` (claude-global-memory). If any has changes, commit and push it the same way, so the other machine's `/dev-sync` picks it up.\n- **Push `global-memory` even on a light session.** `daily-backup` pushes it nightly, which is too late for a same-day handoff: work the Mac in the afternoon, sit down at Windows that evening, and anything written to MEMORY.md or handoffs is still only on the Mac. Pushing it here is what makes memory available to the other machine tonight rather than tomorrow. It is the only repo that carries memory across, so treat an unpushed `global-memory` the same as an unpushed project repo: it blocks \"clear to sign off\".\n- Nothing to commit → say so in one line, move on.\n\n## Step 3 — Memory verification\n\n- Check `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` — must be empty. If not, surface contents and fix the hook issue if obvious.\n- Confirm today's transcript file exists at `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/<today>.md` and was modified within the last few hours. On a Cowork-only day it may be absent; say so, it is not an error.\n- Run the dev-docs harvest so repo docs reach memsearch: `bash ~/.claude/scripts/harvest-dev-docs.sh` (Mac) or `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $HOME\\.claude\\scripts\\harvest-dev-docs.ps1` (Windows). It copies every repo's `docs/*.md` into `~/.claude/global-memory/dev-docs/`, which is on the index list. One line of output; do this after Step 4 so today's STATUS is included.\n- If this session produced a durable decision or new active thread, update `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (respect the 2,500-char cap; consolidate before adding) or write a Tier A memory file per the memory-system rules in CLAUDE.md.\n- **Tier A does not cross machines — mirror it or lose it.** Project memory lives under a slug built from the project's absolute path, and that path differs on the Mac and the Windows laptop, so a Tier A file written today is not auto-injected tomorrow on the other machine. Global memory and the repo both cross; Tier A does not. So for every Tier A fact written this session, ask: is this durable and project-scoped? If yes, it must also land in the repo — `docs/STATUS.md` for current state, `docs/DECISIONS.md` for a decision and its reasoning, the dated handoff for the detail. Treat Tier A as a fast local cache and the repo as the record. If a fact only ever exists in Tier A, assume the other machine will never see it.\n\n## Step 4 — Cross-harness handoff\n\nThis is the **canonical durable handoff** — the one future sessions and other harnesses read. (For a mid-session, chat-only summary before `/clear`, that's the `session-handoff` skill; the mattpocock `handoff` skill writes to the OS temp dir and is not durable — don't rely on it for cross-session state.)\n\nIf real work happened this session (not just Q&A), write a handoff to\n`<repo>/docs/handoffs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md` (or `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` if no repo fits):\n\n- What was being worked on and why\n- Decisions made (with the reasoning that would be lost)\n- Exact file paths of everything created or modified this session\n- Next actions, most important first\n- Anything mid-flight (open questions, awaited inputs)\n\nPlain markdown, no harness-specific syntax — Codex, Antigravity, or a fresh Claude session must be able to resume from it. Commit it with the Step 2 commit when in a repo.\n\n**Always, in every repo touched (even for light sessions): update `docs/STATUS.md`.** It is the file the next session reads first, on either machine, in any harness. Rewrite the sections in place, keep it short:\n\n- `## Current state` — two to five lines on where things stand today\n- `## Next` — most important first. Item 1 is one concrete action (a command to run or a file to open), not a theme: it is the line `~/.claude/scripts/where-was-i.py` shows Eric the next morning\n- `## Open questions` — decisions waiting on Eric, each one written out in full as its own top-level bullet: the question itself and enough context to answer it without opening another file. Never a pointer (\"See PLAN.md\"): `/startup` reads this section across every repo with `where-was-i.py --decisions`, and a pointer gives no question to answer. Mark answered items `ANSWERED <date>: …` or strike them through (`~~…~~`) so they drop off that list. Leave the section empty, not \"-\", when nothing is waiting.\n- `## Gotchas` — anything that will bite the next session\n\nThen run `python ~/.claude/scripts/where-was-i.py --decisions --days 1`. It prints exactly what tomorrow's `/startup` will show as \"Decisions waiting on you\". Every decision this session left open must appear there as a readable question; if one is missing or shows as a pointer, fix that repo's `## Open questions` before the commit.\n\nThe dated handoff file is the detailed record; STATUS.md is the summary. Do Step 4 before the Step 2 commit so both land in the same push.\n\n**Keep the resume cards true (PM system design Decision 9).** The live board shows each project as a card read from STATUS and the registry; if a card doesn't make sense tonight, it won't in six weeks. Run `python <dir of portfolio_json>/landed.py scan --days 1` (about a minute), then `python <dir of portfolio_json>/portfolio.py card-check`. It prints the card of every registry project touched today, on either machine, with its gaps. Fix each gap before the Step 2 commit:\n\n- `STATUS has no entry dated <today>`: add `- <today>: <what happened>` under the repo's Done / Current state.\n- `STATUS has no Next item`: put one concrete action first under `## Next`.\n- `no next step (next_action)` (Drive-only row): set it on the board or with `portfolio.py add-update <name> \"<note>\" --next-action \"<step>\"`.\n- `needs a receiver`: `portfolio.py set-receiver <name> \"<who>\"` (a name, or `me: <why>`). Ask Eric when it is not obvious from the session; never invent one.\n- `touched but marked <status>`: revive it (`portfolio.py set-status <name> active`) or say in STATUS why a retired project was touched.\n\nRe-run `card-check` after the fixes. Its output is the last section of the report (**Cards:**); trim to projects with work today if it is long, but never drop one that still has a gap.\n\n## Step 5 — Verdict\n\nEnd with a single short report:\n\n```\n## Closeout — <date>\n\n**Committed & pushed:** <repos + one-line summary, or \"nothing to commit\">\n**STATUS.md:** updated in <repos>\n**Memory:** captured ✓ / issue found: <what> / skipped (Cowork, no hooks)\n**Handoff:** <path, or \"not needed — no substantive work\">\n**Registry drift:** <N items, or \"none\"> (`portfolio.py crosswalk`)\n**Still open:** <numbered list, or \"none\">\n\nClear to sign off. / Not clear: <the one thing blocking>.\n\n**Cards:** <`portfolio.py card-check` output, after the Step 4 fixes>\n```\n\nThe cards come last on purpose: they are what the next session, on either machine, picks up from.\n\n\"Clear to sign off\" means: every touched repo is pushed (or is a declared local-only repo). If anything is unpushed, the verdict is \"Not clear\" and the unpushed repo is the one thing blocking.\n\nEvery file created or modified during the session must appear somewhere in the report with its full path — \"where did this save?\" should never need to be asked.\n\n**Where everything landed.** Build that list from the ledger, not from memory: the Step 4 `landed.py scan --days 1` already refreshed it (it reads git in every DEV repo and file times on Drive and the vault, so files written by scripts and shell copies are included); run `landed.py show --days 1`. Put its project-grouped output in the report under **Landed:** (trim to the projects this session touched if it is long). The ledger lives in `~/.claude/global-memory/landed/` and is pushed with global-memory, so `/landed` on the other machine shows it too.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Push every touched DEV repo. Do not leave the laptop with unpushed work; the other machine pulls from GitHub, not from this disk.\n- Never skip Step 3 — memory capture failing silently is the user's top recurring worry.\n- If a check fails and the fix is quick and safe (restage a file, clear a stale lock), fix it and note it. If the fix is risky, list it under \"Still open\" instead.\n- Total output should fit on one screen. The user is leaving; brevity is the deliverable.\n"}]},"isMeta":true,"turnCompanion":true,"uuid":"18d4f5de-3d49-497f-b1a7-02daa2ed8839","timestamp":"2026-10-05T13:44:11.356Z","sourceToolUseID":"toolu_01Q7wRpZXNtUmencUj1dM9kq","session_id":"67004755-6eb9-4324-a41f-97820a746f73","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"67004755-6eb9-4324-a41f-97820a746f73","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
